  "diagnosis_text": "The ping fails, but ONOS reports all links on the path active. On s1, I found a high-priority rule that drops frames specifically from h1\u2019s MAC to h5\u2019s MAC. I\u2019ll check its counter during another probe and test whether other traffic can reach h5.\n**Fault localised to a high-priority drop rule on s1**, on the path:\n`h1 \u2192 s4 \u2192 s2 \u2192 s1 \u2192 s3 \u2192 s6 \u2192 h5`.\n\nEvidence:\n- h1 \u2192 h5 failed both ping tests: **100% loss**.\n- On s1, flow **`49821073372291320`**, table 0, priority **50000**, matches:\n  - Source MAC `00:00:00:00:00:01` (h1)\n  - Destination MAC `00:00:00:00:00:05` (h5)\n  - Treatment **`NOACTION`** (drop).\n- During the second three-packet test, its counter increased **7 \u2192 10 packets**, with **294 additional bytes**, directly tying the probes to this rule.\n- It overrides the priority-41000 forwarding rule that would send h5 traffic out **s1 port 2 toward s3**.\n- h2 \u2192 h5 succeeded **3/3**, using the same inter-switch path. ONOS also reports those links active.\n\n**Diagnosis:** selective forwarding-policy blockage at s1, not a general path outage. The drop matches all Ethernet traffic from h1 to h5, not just ICMP.\n\n**Next step:** review this rule and its owning ONOS application, `org.coms6200.experiment`; have the operator correct or remove it if unintended, then retest. I have not changed the network.",
